eld-3/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" "q-342416/field-3/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" "q-342416/field-3/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342416/field-4/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" :answer/id #uuid "518066db-d9fe-59c1-aa1a-febc5d2439f7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342416/field-4/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" :answer/id #uuid "420d5257-7ec2-5759-b45f-f9a4d23dc084" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342416/field-4/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" :answer/id #uuid "eaae1990-82da-5722-9700-50708fadad04" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342416/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "dd110000-e9ff-5a62-83c8-17d5dd053b5a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-342416/field-4/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" :answer-field/choices ["q-342416/field-4/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" "q-342416/field-4/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" "q-342416/field-4/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342416" :question/id #uuid "fea7ff9b-65a7-5f89-87f2-04b4e9266d72" :question/math-academy-id "q-342416" :question/problem "$$\nx(x-1)(x+3){y}^{″}+(x+3){y}^{′}+x(x-1)y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=2$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=-3$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=1$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-1)(x+3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-1)(x+3){y}^{″}+(x+3){y}^{′}+x(x-1)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x(x-1)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x+3}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x(x-1)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x+3}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x-1)=0,$ which occurs at $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x+3=0,$ which occurs at $x=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=-3,\\,x=0,\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-3,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+3)P(x)=(x+3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-1)},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-3,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}Q(x)=(x+3)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3}=x+3,\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=-3.$ Therefore, $x=-3$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-1)}=\\frac{1}{x-1},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ and the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=0.$ Therefore, $x=0$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-1)P(x)=(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-1)}=\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=1,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}Q(x)=(x-1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=1.$ Therefore, $x=1$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342416/field-1" "q-342416/field-2" "q-342416/field-3" "q-342416/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71f8b1c9-7856-52fa-8990-a83fda9ef2a9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342416"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342642/field-1/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" :answer/id #uuid "e96e2cac-87c9-5fe7-9807-c473a3c3dd0c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342642/field-1/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" :answer/id #uuid "e9d8c7b4-9a31-5999-8f7a-ed9b6a90a0cb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342642/field-1/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" :answer/id #uuid "799bdad1-c5cb-5f3e-a5ca-5c4474c2c822" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342642/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf6df1cb-fde7-53ff-a280-7bb406b01e83" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-342642/field-1/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" :answer-field/choices ["q-342642/field-1/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" "q-342642/field-1/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" "q-342642/field-1/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342642/field-2/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" :answer/id #uuid "f49e6633-939d-5778-b47e-249c0b0624f0" :answer/t