3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-342642/field-3/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" :answer-field/choices ["q-342642/field-3/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" "q-342642/field-3/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" "q-342642/field-3/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342642/field-4/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" :answer/id #uuid "3a6c8b16-4315-5e89-b666-81c0987b2d1c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342642/field-4/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" :answer/id #uuid "b47a1e07-feaa-515c-b4ae-a6936cab73ae" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342642/field-4/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" :answer/id #uuid "fb94b512-6709-5b07-bf17-15be8707fc42" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342642/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "a4f29233-9cab-5f2e-93ae-d61d92e02737" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-342642/field-4/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" :answer-field/choices ["q-342642/field-4/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" "q-342642/field-4/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" "q-342642/field-4/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342642" :question/id #uuid "815bad48-989e-5078-af03-3c8c64dddf2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-342642" :question/problem "$$\nx(x-2)(x+1){y}^{″}+x(x+1){y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=1$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=-1$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=2$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-2)(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-2)(x+1){y}^{″}+x(x+1){y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x-2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}(x-2)(x+1)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}(x-2)(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x-2=0,$ which occurs at $x=2.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where ${x}^{3}(x-2)(x+1)=0,$ which occurs at $x=0,$ $x=2,$ and $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,x=2,\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ while the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}(x-2)(x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x(x-2)(x+1)},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=0.$ Thus, $x=0$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=2,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-2)P(x)=(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2}=1,\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=2,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}Q(x)=(x-2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}(x-2)(x+1)}=\\frac{x-2}{{x}^{3}(x+1)},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=2.$ Therefore, $x=2$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=-1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)P(x)=(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-1,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}Q(x)=(x+1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}(x-2)(x+1)}=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{3}(x-2)},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=-1.$ Therefore, $x=-1$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342642/field-1" "q-342642/field-2" "q-342642/field-3" "q-342642/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71f8b1c9-7856-52fa-8990-a83fda9ef2a9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342642"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
